+## Why the URL is validated before it's fetched
+
+`deployHookUrl` is a plain text field an author fills in, and customers will
+soon be editing it themselves. Publishing fetches whatever it holds, so
+without a check that field is an open door to this server's internal
+network: point it at a private IP, a link-local address, or a cloud
+metadata endpoint and the publish flow becomes a proxy into places the
+author was never meant to reach.
+
+Before every call, `triggerDeployHook` runs `assertSafeDeployHookUrl`, which:
+
+- requires a well-formed `https:` URL (no `http:`, `file:`, or anything
+  else),
+- rejects `localhost`, `*.localhost`, and `*.internal` hostnames outright,
+- resolves the hostname and rejects the request if _any_ resolved A/AAAA
+  address falls in loopback, private, link-local (this is where
+  `169.254.169.254` and its cloud-provider equivalents live), or multicast
+  space.
+
+This runs immediately before the fetch it guards rather than being cached
+from, say, when the URL was saved — resolution is what actually determines
+where the request goes, and a cached answer could go stale by the time the
+request fires. The fetch itself also disables redirects (`redirect:
+"manual"`) and caps how long it can hang (`AbortSignal.timeout`), so a
+validated URL can't hand control to an unvalidated one via a 3xx response,
+and a hung endpoint can't stall a publish indefinitely.
+
+A rejected URL short-circuits before any retry — retrying an unsafe address
+doesn't make it safe — and reports back exactly like an exhausted retry does:
+`{ ok: false }`, logged at `error` since there's nowhere else on the API side
+to send it.
+
